Route B (selfhost) G3.7 -- allow ANY NUMBER of REQUIRED POSITIONAL parameters on
a dynamic-module entry point.

Patch 0004 relaxed the upstream "static no-argument" contract to zero-or-one, so
that a replacement for an INSTANCE method could name its receiver as an ordinary
first parameter -- and it capped there deliberately, so "relax the restriction"
could not quietly become a general arity change before anyone had measured one.

This is that general change, measured on its own. A target method with its own
parameters lowers to `f(Receiver self, T1 a, ...)`, so the cap at one is exactly
what made the largest measured slice of real methods unpatchable: 33.2 % of
structural reach, and parameters appear in 6 of 10 real patches -- more than
privacy, which is why the two were measured separately.

STILL REFUSED, each for its own reason, and each pinned by a probe arm:
  non-static            an entry point is resolved without a receiver
  type parameters       generic instantiation is not carried by the payload
  named parameters      the ArgumentsDescriptor's names must match; unexercised
  optional positionals  their defaults live in the AOT function the replacement
                        stands in for, and nothing carries them across -- which
                        is why the test is requiredParameterCount ==
                        positionalParameters.length and not a bare length check

No receiver intrinsic and no VM change: the AOT call site already pushes
(receiver, a, b, ...) per its own ArgumentsDescriptor. What needed measuring was
whether the interpreted entry point binds them positionally as they arrive, and
it does.

Contract pinned by probes/c_entrypoint_arity.sh (8/8, including the two new arms
three_positional=allow and optional_positional=refuse). Behaviour proven end to
end on host by probes/g37_param_abi.sh (4/4): a one-parameter and a
two-parameter target patched and executed through the real producer path, with
the named and optional arms refused at patch time against IDENTICAL release
bytes. The analyzer refuses exactly what this contract refuses (analysisVersion
8), so a rejection never migrates from patch time to compile time.

Applies on top of 0004.
